<body>
<h1>Unit 4: Dynamics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Dynamics supplies the causes. Newton's three laws are the whole of classical mechanics for point masses, but the ways they are applied - free-body diagrams, variable mass, self-adjusting friction - decide whether an answer survives contact with reality.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The first law defines an inertial frame: one in which a free body keeps constant velocity, and the second law then reads F = ma.</li><li>F = ma is the constant-mass form. For a changing mass the correct statement is F = dp/dt, which is what lets a rocket accelerate.</li><li>Third-law pairs act on different bodies, are equal and opposite, and share the same line of interaction.</li><li>Mass measures inertia and is independent of g; weight is the gravitational force mg, so weight changes with location while mass does not.</li><li>A free-body diagram isolates one body and contains only interactions, never 'forces of motion'.</li><li>Static friction is self-adjusting, 0 &lt;= f &lt;= mu N; only at impending slip does it reach mu N, which is why pulling a box at constant speed needs less force than starting it.</li><li>Newton's second law is a rate law: the force at an instant fixes the change of momentum, so a larger force acting for a shorter time can deliver exactly the same impulse.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>F = ma assumes constant mass in an inertial frame; rockets, raindrops gaining mass from vapour, and chains picking up links all violate it.</li><li>Textbook problems neglect air resistance and buoyancy; both become dominant for light bodies, fine dust and falling feathers.</li><li>The classic 'lift and the Earth' third-law discussion fails because the force on the person and the force on the ground are not a third-law pair: the true partner of the person-Newton force is the person-Earth force.</li><li>Friction coefficients are empirical, depend on surface condition, cleanliness, humidity and sliding speed, and are not universal constants of the two materials.</li><li>Newton's laws assume an inertial frame and classical, instantaneous action; they hold to about one part in 10^10 of the speed of light and stop being fundamental at relativistic and quantum scales.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Newton's gravitational constant (exact)</td><td>6.67430e-11 m^3 kg^-1 s^-2</td></tr><tr><td>Earth's mass</td><td>5.972e24 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Force needed to accelerate 1 kg at 1 g</td><td>about 9.81 N</td></tr><tr><td>Impulse of a car decelerating from 50 m/s in 0.05 s</td><td>about 250 N s per kg of mass</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Do smooth, globally defined solutions of the three-dimensional Navier-Stokes equations exist and remain smooth?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The Clay Mathematics Institute announced an apparent solution in September 2026 and states that the work is still to be analysed and evaluated; it should be presented as announced, not as settled.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can the transition from laminar to turbulent flow be predicted from first principles?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Direct numerical simulation still reaches only moderate Reynolds numbers, so the transition statistics remain an active research target.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Draw free-body diagrams for a lift accelerating upward with total mass m and explain both readings of the word 'weight'.</li><li>Show that the third law does not prevent a rocket from accelerating: track the momentum carried away by the exhaust.</li><li>Estimate the terminal velocity of a raindrop using a quadratic drag law and compare the estimate with observation.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/4-introduction-to-dynamics-newtons-laws-of-motion">OpenStax: Newton's laws</a></li><li><a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium/navier-stokes-equation/">Clay: the Navier-Stokes problem</a></li><li><a href="https://www.claymath.org/news/navier-stokes-announcement/">Clay: Navier-Stokes announcement, 11 September 2026</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-3-newtons-second-law">OpenStax: Newton's Second Law</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
